What:		/dev/v4l-subdev* (CAMSS gen2 CSID), V4L2_EVENT_FRAME_SYNC
Date:		October 2026
Contact:	A081 mainline port
Description:
		Subscribe with id=0 on the CSID subdevice. Notifications are
		available only with a single enabled RDI source link, so the
		media graph identifies the stream without a private event ID.
		Multiple enabled RDI links are rejected at subscription time;
		notifications are suppressed if the graph becomes ambiguous.

		frame_sequence counts observed input-SOF interrupts, starts
		at zero on STREAMON, and wraps at 32 bits. It is not a sensor
		frame counter or a match for a video buffer's sequence. IRQ
		coalescing and event queue overflow can lose observations.
		The event timestamp is CLOCK_MONOTONIC notification time,
		not latched hardware SOF and not exposure start. This event
		can be used to wait until each camera has started receiving.

What:		/sys/module/qcom_camss/parameters/frame_headers
Date:		October 2026
Contact:	A081 mainline port
Description:
		Read-only boolean module parameter, default Y in the cammeta
		baseline (the validated hrtimer baseline default is N).
		Load with frame_headers=0 to disable the SM8250 VFE480
		per-buffer DMA header path. Each video buffer gets a separate
		128-byte coherent allocation in the image DMA domain. No
		image format, stride, plane layout or payload is changed.

		The header holds a 56-bit QTimer SOF value in word[1]<<24 |
		word[0]>>8, as decoded by the downstream bus-v3 driver. Other
		bits are uninterpreted. The configured cookie is software
		enqueue order; no header frame ID or cookie echo is assumed.

		Timestamp validation requires an exact match between the
		header SOF and a retained CSID SOF/EOF pair. Empty,
		overwritten or unmatched timestamps mark the
		buffer ERROR. V4L2 timestamps retain EOF source semantics;
		timestamps on ERROR buffers must not be used for tracking.
		Setting N preserves the earlier CSID EOF timestamp path but
		cannot provide valid per-buffer timing metadata.

		With timestamp_debug=1, existing frame diagnostics also
		include buffer index, configured cookie, header SOF, the
		first four raw little-endian header words, clock-read bracket
		and lookup status. These printk records are diagnostic, not
		a stable per-frame metadata interface. Two-slot image/header
		FIFO pairing and the two SOF latch stages matched in a bounded
		A081 four-stream run: 160 frames per stream, eight buffers each
		reused twenty times, with every header SOF equal to its CSID
		SOF and all lookups valid. This covers normal capture only,
		not delayed interrupts, starvation or recovery. These logs
		are not a production SOF
		metadata interface. Neither SOF nor EOF is sensor exposure
		start. Evidence: reports/device/camera-barrier-r1/ in
		the A081 port workspace.

What:		/dev/video* named msm_vfeN_videoM_timing, format QCFT
Date:		October 2026
Contact:	A081 mainline port
Description:
		A081 port ABI, not accepted upstream. Two bounded four-stream
		sessions validated 960 records against 1280 images, including
		normal close/reopen; see camera-metadata-r1 results. SM8250 RDI image nodes have an ancillary-linked
		V4L2_BUF_TYPE_META_CAPTURE node named IMAGE_ENTITY_timing.
		Discover by media entities/ancillary links; video node numbers
		change when metadata nodes are registered. This node supports
		MMAP streaming only. Start its queue before the image stream,
		and drain it alongside image buffers. It never starts sensors
		or controls the image stream. Stop both queues per session.

		QCFT is the port-local fourcc defined in
		linux/qcom-camss-timing.h. A payload is one native-endian,
		216-byte qcom_camss_frame_timing version 2 record with identical
		ARM32/ARM64 field layout. Reserved fields are zero. Metadata
		sequence and timestamp mirror the associated image buffer's
		sequence and MONOTONIC EOF timestamp (metadata retains ns;
		V4L2 timeval has microsecond precision). The image's format,
		stride and payload are unchanged. Buffer index and software
		enqueue cookie are included; neither is a sensor frame number.

		stream_id is a nonzero generation incremented at each attempted
		image STREAMON, local to this node and module lifetime. Join
		image and metadata by stream session and image_sequence, and
		check image_buffer_index. Drain/stop metadata between sessions;
		do not join a restarted image stream to old queued metadata.
		The CSID/RDI identify the configured route, not a hard-coded
		physical sensor. Resolve the active media graph to obtain the
		board slot. Sequence numbers need not match across cameras.

		Raw SOF/EOF are 19.2MHz QTimer CSI packet events. The separate
		DMA header selects the exact retained SOF/EOF pair for this
		image. VALID requires a nonzero matched header, valid EOF
		progress and a clock-read bracket below 5000 ns. Otherwise the
		metadata buffer is ERROR and mapped SOF/EOF fields are zero.
		The caller must reject ERROR image or metadata buffers, missing
		VALID, nonzero lookup_status or missing/mismatched records.
		The first four header words are preserved uninterpreted apart
		from SOF; their other fields are not promised sensor counters.

		CLOCK_SNAPSHOT indicates ktime_get_snapshot_id(CLOCK_MONOTONIC)
		returned a valid ARM_ARCH_COUNTER sample: sample_qtimer and
		sample_monotonic_ns refer to the exact same clocksource read,
		protected by the timekeeper sequence. clock_bracket_ns is zero
		for this exact pair; it is not a measurement of call duration.
		The BOOTTIME offset is read before/after and must be unchanged.
		An invalid snapshot, different clocksource or changed offset
		clears CLOCK_SNAPSHOT and VALID and sets clock_bracket_ns to
		UINT_MAX. No delayed read is accepted by widening a bound.
		Older producers without CLOCK_SNAPSHOT used a MONOTONIC
		read bracket below 5000 ns and its midpoint. Consumers can
		accept either provenance with the same strict validity checks.
		Each event maps as sample_ns minus
		floor((sample_qtimer - event_qtimer)*10000/192), then uses the
		same kernel BOOTTIME offset. Clock pairing does not establish
		physical sensor accuracy or exposure latency. BOOTTIME values
		already include suspended time: do not add a second native-sensor
		QTimer offset. Suspend recovery is not validated. No timestamp
		field claims to be exposure start or midpoint.

		If no metadata buffer is queued, image capture continues.
		metadata_dropped counts these omissions while the metadata
		queue is streaming, modulo 32 bits, reset on image STREAMON.
		Missing records are never synthesized or reused. Consumer
		backpressure on this queue does not stall the image stream.
		Version 2 appends 56 bytes to the original 160-byte prefix,
		with the original reserved words still zero. PATTERN_ACTIVE
		indicates frame scheduling; EXPOSURE_VALID requires exact
		header/SOF matching and a successful sensor group launch for
		this target frame. exposure_status reports the scheduling result
		(zero or negative errno). Without EXPOSURE_VALID do not use the appended
		exposure or phase for tracking. CSI SOF remains a packet time.

		sensor_sequence is the low 32 bits of an internal 64-bit SOF
		IRQ count, independent of image_sequence. exposure_phase is
		computed internally before truncation. exposure_ns is derived
		from programmed integration lines and the sensor pixel clock.
		control_apply_sof_qtimer is the source SOF two frames before
		the target; control_applied_boottime_ns is group completion.
		phase_anchor_sof_qtimer identifies the actual reference frame.

What:		/dev/v4l-subdev* (A081 SM8250 CSID), VIDIOC_QCOM_CAMSS_EXPOSURE_PATTERN
Date:		October 2026
Contact:	A081 mainline port
Description:
		Explicit 88-byte version-1 pattern ioctl. Disabled by default.
		Only supported with a running, single-RDI OV7251 pipeline.
		Three steps specify integration lines, analogue gain and an
		identical frame length. All reserved fields must be zero.
		Before arm, match four real QCFT hardware SOFs from the same
		physical frame, then pass each sensor its own exact anchor SOF
		and a common phase_at_anchor. The anchor must still exist in
		that stream's 64-frame history. Future timestamps and guessed
		video sequence offsets are not accepted.

		period_qtimer is checked against eight recent hardware SOF
		intervals. All intervals and the supplied period must lie
		within 5 percent of their median; the kernel then uses that
		measured median. An unstable history returns EAGAIN, a wrong
		period ERANGE, and an expired/missing anchor ESTALE.

		A real SOF wakes sleepable CCI work for target frame +2, matching
		the original Android CRM sensor pipeline delay. Each CSID has a
		dedicated high-priority unbound workqueue, preserving per-sensor
		serialization while allowing four CCI requests to progress away
		from the shared IRQ CPU. No timer drives the pattern. The worker must complete before the next SOF;
		missing/coalesced IRQs, late completion or I2C errors latch
		invalid exposure metadata until disable/rearm or stream restart.
		The initial two-frame pipeline has invalid exposure metadata.
		E/G/VBLANK are owned by the scheduler while active. Sensor group
		operations use an internal subdevice command, not a user ioctl.
		Disable releases ownership but does not restore a static exposure;
		configure static controls explicitly or stop the image stream.
		A real sensor I2C group error additionally requires power-on reset.
